e groups are spending against the incumbent in a favorable midterm environment"], "rationale": "The Republican incumbent enters the rematch with a commanding fundraising advantage in a district that has resisted Democrats for three decades, and most raters place it in the Solid or Safe Republican column. Democratic outside spending and a strong midterm environment give Conroy a plausible path, but not enough to close an eight-point gap.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling showing Conroy within single digits, or a stronger-than-expected national Democratic wave, would make this genuinely competitive.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Michael Baumgartner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Michael Baumgartner seeks a second term representing Washington's 5th District, facing Democrat Carmela Conroy in a rematch of their 2024 contest. The district has not elected a Democrat in three decades and leans Republican by about five points, and Baumgartner holds a large financial edge, with roughly $954,000 on hand to Conroy's $74,000. Most professional ratings place the race in the Solid or Safe Republican column, though two call it Likely R and two call it Lean R.\n\nPolling is sparse and dated: the only available surveys come from Democratic-leaning firms and showed Baumgartner ahead by about six points in the spring. Democratic outside groups have spent against the incumbent, and national party committees have flagged the district as a pickup opportunity in a favorable midterm year. Prediction markets give Conroy roughly a one-in-five chance. The race remains likely, but not out of reach, for Baumgartner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0968, "margin": -8.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1087, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0951, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0945, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26